<h2>Testing Multiple Sectional Configurations</h2>
<p>Dividing a model into smaller sections can make packing and handling easier, but each connection introduces a joint that must work within the assembled display.</p>
<p>For Aviation Garage, we evaluated multiple ways to divide both the building and its base. The goal was to identify where separation could improve transport and handling without compromising the model’s appearance or structural integrity.</p>
<p>The building and base were assessed separately, allowing each component’s construction to reflect its role in the finished display.</p>

<h2>A Two-Part Base and a One-Piece Building</h2>
<p>Following these tests, we chose to divide the base into two sections while keeping the building structure in one piece.</p>
<p>The sectional base provided flexibility for packing and handling. Keeping the building intact avoided joints through the architecture and preserved a continuous presentation of its form and details.</p>
<p>This approach balanced transport requirements with presentation quality, placing divisions where they offered the most practical benefit.</p>
