<h2>Keeping Setup Simple Without Lighting</h2>
<p>Integrated lighting was excluded because the model was intended for repeated assembly and disassembly. Electrical components and connections would have added setup complexity and introduced additional points of wear over its lifespan.</p>
<p>Leaving out lighting reduced the components the client’s team would need to connect, check and maintain between events. The presentation instead relied on the model’s architectural form and detail.</p>

<h2>Designing for Repeated Event Use</h2>
<p>A trade show model needs to remain practical beyond its first presentation. Transport, setup, dismantling and storage all influence the construction decisions made during development.</p>
<p>For Aviation Garage, testing different sectional configurations helped establish where separation was useful and where continuity mattered most. The decision to omit lighting followed the same principle: consider how each feature affects the model throughout repeated use.</p>
<p>Together, these decisions shaped a display designed around both architectural communication and the client’s handling needs.</p>
